1
00:00:00,810 --> 00:00:03,520
$188 less a month?

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:07,260
It'll cost you $3,759.

3
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:08,250
Here's the math.

4
00:00:10,310 --> 00:00:13,020
Say a dealer quotes a $40,000 loan

5
00:00:13,190 --> 00:00:14,740
at 8.1% interest.

6
00:00:15,130 --> 00:00:16,210
Over 60 months,

7
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:18,460
that's about $813 a month.

8
00:00:18,690 --> 00:00:20,100
Stretch it to 84,

9
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:22,340
and it drops to $625.

10
00:00:24,070 --> 00:00:27,040
$188 LESS. Feels like a win.

11
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:28,610
But look at the interest.

12
00:00:28,930 --> 00:00:29,820
60 months:

13
00:00:30,020 --> 00:00:31,450
about $8,800.

14
00:00:31,730 --> 00:00:32,740
84 months:

15
00:00:32,930 --> 00:00:34,650
about $12,500.

16
00:00:35,030 --> 00:00:35,840
Same car.

17
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:37,800
Nearly $4,000 MORE.

18
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:40,470
A longer loan means
more months of interest,

19
00:00:40,570 --> 00:00:41,680
and slower equity.

20
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:46,850
Edmunds found over 91% of people who
rolled old debt into a new loan

21
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:49,290
chose terms longer than six years.

22
00:00:49,540 --> 00:00:50,610
So before you sign,

23
00:00:50,810 --> 00:00:52,150
ask for the TOTAL interest,

24
00:00:52,290 --> 00:00:53,790
not just the monthly payment.

25
00:00:54,260 --> 00:00:57,000
Six years or less is
a good ceiling to consider.

26
00:00:57,250 --> 00:00:58,330
This is education,

27
00:00:58,580 --> 00:00:59,900
not financial advice.

28
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:02,000
Subscribe so you don't miss the next one.

29
00:01:02,270 --> 00:01:03,340
See you next time.
